  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 1209 AM MST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nThe mid part of the week is forecast to start with a trough digging \ninto the Plains and East Coast. This trough is forecast to bring in \na weak cold front that should keep temperature steady with lows in \nthe 30s/40s and highs in the 60s. Winds are forecast to pick up a \nbit Wednesday as the front passes through, but with speeds only \naround 10-15 mph. Thursday would then see winds lower back to calm. \nNo precipitation is forecast for either day with dry air in place. \nThe main thing to watch for is if the upper trough digs a bit \nfurther west and doesn't move out as quickly. The main changes would \nthen be cooler temperatures on Thursday with highs in the 50s, wind \nspeeds similar to Wednesday, and much more cloud cover.\n\nFriday and into the weekend is forecast to have an upper ridge move \nin behind the trough. The ridge is looking a bit more amplified in \nguidance compared to yesterday's forecast. While the forecast for \nthe now keeps lows in the 30s/40s and highs in the 60s, it wouldn't \nbe unreasonable for highs to reach the 70s and maybe even the 80s if \nthe ridge is as persistent and amplified as some guidance suggests. \nWinds would favor remaining light and calm as long as the ridge \nmoves over, along with mostly clear to clear skies. One thing to \nkeep an eye on is that some guidance is suggesting a shortwave will \nmove in from the west around late Saturday or Sunday. While a bit \nfar out to put confidence in, it does look like the next chance at \nsome precipitation if it does materialize. The chance is about 10-\n15%. Amounts would likely be light with the dry air in place needing \nto be overcome.",
